  "verbalization": "Cortisone Acetate appears to be linked to Tinea Corporis through a chain of indications involving dermatological conditions. The evidence suggests that Cortisone Acetate is used to treat Tinea Pedis, a fungal infection, and that Tinea Pedis is associated with Econazole, Oxiconazole, or Sulconazole, all of which are antifungal agents. These agents are indicated for Tinea Corporis, a similar fungal infection affecting the skin. The shared indication pathway implies a potential mechanism of action involving antifungal therapy, though the exact mechanism of action for Cortisone Acetate remains unclear. The evidence supports a possible repurposing of Cortisone Acetate for Tinea Corporis, though further research is needed to confirm its efficacy and safety in this context.",
